#### Article 53

1. The Regency Level Children's Forum as referred to in Article 52 paragraph (2) letter a is a forum for children's participation in the Regency area whose members consist of representatives of the Sub-district Level Children's Forum of at least 1 (one) girl and 1 (one) boy.
2. The Regency Level Children's Forum is established by Regent's Decree with a term of office of 2 (two) years.
3. In order for the Regency Level Children's Forum to function properly, a secretariat must be formed.
4. The formation of the secretariat as referred to in paragraph (3) may involve at most 40% (forty percent) of unmarried adults.
5. The involvement of adults as referred to in paragraph (4) is intended to guide and foster new members of the Regency Level Children's Forum.
6. The Regency Regional Apparatus responsible for women's empowerment and child protection as the Supervisor of the Regency Level Children's Forum facilitates the formation of the Regency Level Children's Forum starting from the selection stage, validation of management and guidance in capacity building of members of the Regency Level Children's Forum.

### Paragraph 2

Sub-district Level Children's Forum

#### Article 54

1. The Sub-district Level Children's Forum as referred to in Article 52 paragraph (2) letter b is a forum for children's participation in the sub-district area whose members consist of representatives of the Village Level Children's Forum of at least 1 (one) girl and 1 (one) boy.
2. The Sub-district Level Children's Forum is established by Sub-district Head's Decree with a term of office of 2 (two) years.
3. The Sub-district Government as the Supervisor of the Sub-district Level Children's Forum facilitates the formation of the Sub-district Level Children's Forum starting from the selection stage, validation of management and guidance in capacity building of members of the Sub-district Level Children's Forum.

### Paragraph 3

Village Level Children's Forum

#### Article 55

1. The Village Level Children's Forum as referred to in Article 52 paragraph (2) letter c is a forum for children's participation in the Village area whose members consist of representatives from Neighborhood Associations and Community Associations of at least 1 (one) girl and 1 (one) boy.
2. The Village Level Children's Forum is established by Village Head's Decree with a term of office of 2 (two) years.
3. The Village Government as the Supervisor of the Village Level Children's Forum facilitates the formation of the Village Level Children's Forum starting from the selection stage, validation of management and guidance in capacity building of members of the Village Level Children's Forum.
4. Community institutions existing in the Village have the obligation to accommodate and foster children's participation in development and in advocating for children's rights.

# CHAPTER X

## CHILD-FRIENDLY REGENCY

#### Article 56

1. The Regional Government is obliged to implement a Child-Friendly Regency implementation system in order to fulfill children's rights. (2) The development of a Child-Friendly Regency is carried out based on the following principles:
a. good governance, namely transparency, accountability, participation, information disclosure, and rule of law;
b. non-discrimination, namely not distinguishing ethnicity, race, religion, gender, language, political views, national origin, economic status, physical or psychological condition of the child, or other factors;
c. the best interests of the child, namely making what is best for the child the primary consideration in every policy, program, and activity;
d. the right to life, survival, and development of the child, namely guaranteeing the right to life, survival and development of the child to the maximum extent possible; and
e. respect for the views of the child, namely recognizing and ensuring that every child who has the ability to express their opinion is given the opportunity to express their views freely on all matters affecting them.
2. The implementation apparatus of the Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. development system and public services;
b. Child-Friendly Health Services;
c. Child-Friendly Family;
d. Child-Friendly School; and
e. Child-Friendly Environment;
f. Child-Friendly Islamic Boarding School.
3. The Regional Government in implementing the Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) must pay attention to the Child-Friendly Regency Indicators which include:
a. institutional; and
b. children's rights clusters.
4. Institutional strengthening as referred to in paragraph (4) letter a includes:
a. the existence of laws and regulations and policies for the fulfillment of children's rights;
b. percentage of budget for the fulfillment of children's rights, including budget for institutional strengthening;
c. number of laws and regulations, policies, programs and activities that receive input from Children's Forums and other children's groups;
d. availability of Human Resources (HR) trained in the Convention on the Rights of the Child (CRC) and capable of applying children's rights into policies, programs, and activities;
e. availability of disaggregated child data by gender, age, and sub-district;
f. involvement of community institutions in the fulfillment of children's rights; and
g. involvement of the business sector in the fulfillment of children's rights.

#### Article 57

Children's rights clusters as referred to in Article 56 paragraph (4) letter b which include:
a. Civil rights and freedoms which include:
1\. children's right to identity;
2\. right to protection of identity;
3\. right to express and voice opinions;
4\. right to think, have conscience, religion, and belief;
5\. right to organize and assemble peacefully;
6\. right to protection of private life;
7\. right to access appropriate information; and
8\. right to be free from torture and other cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment or punishment.
b. Rights to family environment and care which include:
1\. parental guidance and responsibility;
2\. children separated from parents;
3\. reunification;
4\. illegal transfer of children;
5\. welfare support;
6\. children forced to be separated from the family environment;
7\. child adoption;
8\. periodic review of placement; and
9\. violence and neglect.
c. Children's rights to basic health and welfare which include:
a. children with disabilities receive access to health services and welfare;
b. health and health services;
c. social security health services and facilities; and
d. standard of living;
d. Children's rights to education, leisure time utilization and cultural activities which include:
1. education;
2. objectives of education; and
3. holiday activities, cultural activities, and sports.
e. Protection for children in special protection which includes:
1\. children in emergency situations;
2\. children in conflict with the law;
3\. children in exploitation situations; and
4\. children belonging to minority and isolated groups.

#### Article 58

1. To make the development of a Child-Friendly Regency more effective, a Child-Friendly Regency Task Force is formed which is established by Regent's Decree.
2. The membership of the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) may come from the Regional Government, children's representatives, business sector, religious leaders, community leaders and other parties as needed.
3. In carrying out the main duties, members of the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force as referred to in paragraph (2) have detailed duties including:
a. coordinating various efforts to develop a Child-Friendly Regency;
b. preparing a Regional Action Plan for Child-Friendly Regency Development;
c. conducting socialization, advocacy and communication on Child-Friendly Regency development;
d. monitoring the implementation of policies, programs and activities in the Regional Action Plan for Child-Friendly Regency Development;
e. conducting evaluation at the end of each year on the implementation of policies, programs and activities in the Regional Action Plan for Child-Friendly Regency Development; and
f. submitting reports to the Regent.
